<doc><doc_main><par id="PAR:named_paragraph" tag="PAR"><par_lbl>¶ 1</par_lbl><par_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>This is a no-markup-markup document, sporting the following cool features</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>:</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_itm><blk_itm_lbl>(1)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Bullet lists</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>:</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_blt><blk_blt_lbl>─</blk_blt_lbl><blk_blt_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Here is one bullet item.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_blt_main></blk_blt><blk_blt><blk_blt_lbl>─</blk_blt_lbl><blk_blt_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Here is another one.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_blt_main></blk_blt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_itm><blk_itm_lbl>(2)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Numbered lists with labels that are either</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_itm><blk_itm_lbl>(a)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>automatic, or</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_itm><blk_itm_lbl>(*)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>custom.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Furthermore, each item can be </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_emph>named</txt_unit_emph><txt_unit_wysiwyg> and </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_emph>referred to</txt_unit_emph><txt_unit_wysiwyg>:</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_itm id="ITM:x" tag="ITM"><blk_itm_lbl>(b)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>The named item can be referred to anywhere in the document.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_itm id="ITM:y" tag="ITM"><blk_itm_lbl>(c)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>When referring to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:x">(b)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> above, any common initial path shared between the location of the referring expression and the location of its referent will be omitted. The same applies with respect to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:z">(æ)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> below.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_itm id="ITM:z" tag="ITM"><blk_itm_lbl>(æ)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Of course, items with custom labels can also be named!</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></blk_itm_main></blk_itm></blk_itm_main></blk_itm><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Due to the feature mentioned in </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:y">(2)(c)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg>, a reference to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:x">(2)(b)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> or </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:z">(2)(æ)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> occurring elsewhere may appear differently.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_itm><blk_itm_lbl>(3)</blk_itm_lbl><blk_itm_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Displayed lines of text suitable for mathematical equations, which are either</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt><blk_dsp><dsp_line id="DSP:one" tag="DSP"><dsp_line_lbl>(a)</dsp_line_lbl><dsp_line_main><txt_unit_wysiwyg>automatically labeled (and named),</txt_unit_wysiwyg></dsp_line_main></dsp_line><dsp_line id="DSP:two" tag="DSP"><dsp_line_lbl>(+)</dsp_line_lbl><dsp_line_main><txt_unit_wysiwyg>custom labeled (and named), or,</txt_unit_wysiwyg></dsp_line_main></dsp_line><dsp_line><dsp_line_main><txt_unit_wysiwyg>unlike </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#DSP:one">(a)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> above, unlabeled.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></dsp_line_main></dsp_line></blk_dsp></blk_itm_main></blk_itm></par_main></par><par><par_lbl>¶ 2</par_lbl><par_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>We may also refer to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:x">1(2)(b)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> or </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#ITM:y">1(2)(c)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> from another paragraph. These references will appear differently than when occurring in </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#PAR:named_paragraph">1</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg>.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></par_main></par><par><par_lbl>¶ 3</par_lbl><par_hdr><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Paragraph header, which is a text block</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>that may contain </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_emph>emph text</txt_unit_emph><txt_unit_wysiwyg> and a reference to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#DSP:one">1(3)(a)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg>.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></par_hdr><par_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Paragraph header, which is a text block</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>that may contain </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_emph>emph text</txt_unit_emph><txt_unit_wysiwyg> and a reference to </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#DSP:one">1(3)(a)</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg>.</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>This is a paragraph with a header. As we shall se in </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_c_ref href="#PAR:named_paragraph_with_header">4</txt_unit_c_ref><txt_unit_wysiwyg> below, paragraphs with headers can also be named.</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></par_main></par><par id="PAR:named_paragraph_with_header" tag="PAR"><par_lbl>¶ 4</par_lbl><par_hdr><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Header of the named paragraph</txt_unit_wysiwyg></par_hdr><par_main><blk_txt><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Header of the named paragraph</txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg> </txt_unit_wysiwyg><txt_unit_wysiwyg>Believe it or not, it actually works!</txt_unit_wysiwyg></blk_txt></par_main></par></doc_main></doc>
